SQS vs SNS vs Amazon MQ – When to Use What?

AWS offers multiple messaging services, and it’s common to get confused about when to use which. Here’s a simple breakdown:

Amazon SQS (Simple Queue Service) :-
Best for: Decoupling microservices, building serverless/event-driven apps.
How it works: Messages are placed in a queue and consumed by workers in order.
Use case examples: Order processing systems, background jobs, async workflows.
Why choose it: Fully managed, scales automatically, guarantees message delivery.

Amazon SNS (Simple Notification Service):-
Best for: Broadcasting or fan-out messaging.
How it works: Publishes messages to multiple subscribers (e.g., Lambda, SQS, HTTP endpoints, email, SMS).
Use case examples: Alerts/notifications, fan-out updates to multiple systems, pub/sub patterns.
Why choose it: Low-latency push notifications, great for real-time alerts and integrations.

 Amazon MQ:-
Best for: Migrating from traditional message brokers (ActiveMQ, RabbitMQ).
How it works: Managed message broker that supports industry-standard APIs and protocols (JMS, AMQP, STOMP, MQTT, OpenWire).
Use case examples: Enterprise apps that rely on messaging middleware or need minimal code changes to migrate.
Why choose it: Smooth migration path, no need to rewrite applications, supports hybrid cloud setups.

When to use What:
Use SQS when you need reliable, scalable queues for decoupling.
Use SNS when you need to broadcast messages or trigger multiple subscribers.
Use Amazon MQ when migrating legacy systems or when your apps depend on standard messaging protocols.
